# Architecture Health LLM Guide

## Purpose

Use this command as the local architecture health collector: boundaries, shape, map, clusters, pressure, and coherence.

## When To Run

- Before starting a non-trivial implementation.
- After touching imports, module seams, or feature structure.
- Before finalizing work when you need one architecture-oriented detail report.

## How To Read The Result

- Use `--v` for quick status, `--vv` for the normal preflight, and `--vvv` when module-level detail should be visible.
- Boundary and shape findings are blockers when marked as errors.
- Unknown files are classification signals, not automatic failures.
- Hotspots point to module-size or dependency-pressure areas worth treating carefully.
- Pressure clusters show likely blast radius and shared-code gravity.
- Pressure and coherence findings are attention signals; mention them when they affect the task.

## Examples

- If boundaries or shape report errors, fix those before summarizing the work.
- If unknown files appear, inspect local rules before moving or editing them.
- If a high-pressure cluster is involved, prefer smaller commits and rerun checks often.
- If health passes but shows attention findings, mention the residual risk briefly.

## Agent Tips

- Use this as the architecture drilldown after root `enclosure health`.
- Follow up with `enclosure architecture boundaries` when changing dependency direction.
- Follow up with `enclosure architecture shape` after editing imports or public code shape.
- Follow up with `enclosure architecture map` when ownership or placement is unclear.
- Follow up with `enclosure architecture clusters` before broad refactors or risky dependency work.
- Follow up with `enclosure architecture pressure` before reducing prop drilling or public API surface.
- Follow up with `enclosure architecture coherence` before streamlining related modules.
- Do not treat hotspot ranking as ownership policy; policy comes from the contract.
